pleased to say that the vaccine  deployment minister nadim zahawi joins  us once again on the program mr zahari  good to have you on once again and  clearly  lots and lots to talk about uh very  specific to your brief but but i wonder  if we can start with  at that which a number of the papers  appear to have picked up on this morning  that there was a meeting yesterday of  the prime minister's senior ministers  at which it was discussed uh that  whether or not  we need to see tougher restrictions or  indeed tougher enforcement  of existing restrictions just talk to us  a little bit about that thank you very  much uh  neil for having me on so the lockdown is  is  actually pretty severe we're asking  people to stay at home  um don't go out if you have to go out  it's only for exercise  this virus loves social interaction and  we are all  pro social animals and it's the worst  thing we do  is to socialize so only going out for  exercise limited time  stay at home we are concerned that uh  for example in supermarkets uh we need  to make sure people actually wear masks  and  follow the one waste rule in  supermarkets and of course  when they are at capacity to operate  safely people wait uh outside  supermarkets so  uh we don't want to go you know  any tougher because this is a pretty  tough lockdown uh what we need is people  to behave as if they've got the virus  and so that we can  bring this virus under control whilst we  vaccinate  and my role is very much focused on  making sure  that we hit that target of mid february  where we  protect those top four categories where  you know 80 plus percent of the deaths  are coming from  just in terms though you mentioned  several times supermarkets there and  also referenced in the papers is there  some way by which we could be applying  pressure on the supermarkets themselves  to ensure that enforcement of the rules  is a responsibility of the supermarkets  and not just of the individuals  well our plea is to everybody uh each  and every one of us  you know these rules are not boundaries  to be  sort of pushed against these rules are  there to try and  make sure we bring this virus under  control reduce  the pressure on the nhs save lives  that's the really important thing  and as i say this virus loves social  interaction i know it's hard  we're all pro-social animals but just  think  every social interaction you may have  that could be an incident  of transfer transmission of the virus  the supermarkets  have done a tremendous job and i want to  complement and thank them all  for the incredible work they've done and  also coming forward  to help with uh you know offer offers  for  areas where we can use for vaccination  uh  hubs and others but ultimately the most  important thing to do now is to make  sure that actually enforcement  and of course the compliance  with the rules when people are going  into supermarkets are  being adhered to i mean compliance is  clearly  something of an issue anecdotally anyone  who took a drive on saturday or sunday  this weekend may have been slightly  struck by the scenes that they saw  people congregating people grabbing  coffees what do you say to those people  who are who are clearly  exploiting loopholes uh within the  covered 19 regulations we're looking at  pictures from victoria park at the  weekend  people have clearly gone out to take  their exercise and just happen to have  bumped into people  i mean are they not simply displaying  the same common sense that the  government told us that dominic cummings  was  all i would say to people is is this  virus loves  social interaction especially this new  variant which is far more infectious  and i know it's hard it's very hard on  us all because as i said earlier we are  all pro-social we want  you know we it's it's in our dna dare i  say that that we socialize but this is  the worst thing we can do  in terms of helping the virus spread  further  we must must must make sure that we only  go out  when we exercise or obviously to get  some necessary um  shopping done but otherwise stay at home  that is the message  if we all follow that rule stay at home  then we will bring the  virus under control and of course the  vaccinations are really beginning to  ramp up  200 000 a day we've done you know an  incredible job this past week fourth to  the 11th of january you will see later  today  when we publish the figures uh we've had  a a  really significant ramp up in  vaccinations  and we'll do the same and more next week  and the week after  and by mid february we will have  protected those top four categories and  then we keep going  beyond that all night so can we just be  very specific when you say that by  mid-february we will have protected  the most vulnerable categories what do  you mean by that do you mean that by  mid-february we're going to see 14  million people having been vaccinated  because that isn't the direction we're  receiving from downing street this  morning  so the top four categories actually for  the uk is 15 million people  um in england it's about 10 12 million  people  um so we would have offered a  vaccination to all those people  uh to make sure that we can protect them  obviously well that's i'm  sorry minister now that is somewhat  different from what you said a second  ago 40 million people would be protected  now that's not the case as you say  it's 14 million people will have been  offered a vaccine  by the middle of february and providing  us a running total of how many people  have been offered a vaccine but  what use is that i mean that just tells  us how good the royal mail and  the mobile phone companies are  communicating we need to know how many  people are going to be vaccinated by  mid-february so  when will the 14 million people have  received their vaccination  you're absolutely right neil uh and when  you when you offer  a vaccination doesn't mean a royal mail  letter it means actually  the vaccine and the the needle and the  jab is ready for you  and so what you'll see us publishing is  the  total numbers of people being vaccinated  uh not being offered  a vaccine and that's the number to hold  us account to  um okay in terms of the rollout itself  the seven  mass vaccination centers will be music  to the ears of people who are waiting  to get there i i do wonder however the  the 45 minute travel  time what happens if for example  an older person is unable to make that  journey this is not their only shot at  getting a shot is it absolutely right  neil it's very important to remember  that  to this week is the first week of the  rollout of the  uh the vaccination center seven as you  introduced in your piece more next week  more the week after  and yes at the moment the six hundred  thousand letters that have gone out  it are two people 80 year old plus uh  in those top two categories and of  course we'll move down the  categories down to the four top  categories  where the travel time is up to 45  minutes  that will obviously change plus they  have the option not to take  uh that particular route and to wait for  their gp  to vaccinate them so they have other  options  and what i want to get to is to make  sure that every  adult in england has the ability to  vaccinate within 10  mile radius of where they live that is  our absolute focus that is what you'll  see  us do we will roll out more vaccination  centers next week and the  week after we'll bring on the community  pharmacies 200 as the prime minister  uh announced at the press conference a  few days ago so  this is an enormous role at the largest  vaccination program in the history of  this country  but i'm confident that through gps  obviously through hospitals that we've  already got 220 plus hospitals  over a thousand gp primary care  networks by the way who are already  supported by pharmacies as well  then the vaccination centers and of  course pharmacies  you will see the ability for  people to get a vaccine become easier  and easier  in days and weeks to come i i wonder if  we might ask but a couple of categories  of people who have been  looking to secure the vaccine more  quickly than the system currently  allows and police officers on the front  line  throughout all of this and now we  understand some of them a small number a  handful obviously 75 of them  are going to be drafted in to start  driving ambulances is it not now time to  start thinking about  you know making sure that the copper  coppers out there on the front line have  a vaccine  sooner rather than later and also just a  related topic  beginning of december we spoke to fred  banning himself who  suffers from stage four bowel cancer  it is a terminal condition and there are  people in his circumstance  who are asking for the vaccine earlier  than than currently so that they can  in essence extend their time with their  family we were told in december the  government was looking at this  i'm just wondering what the state of  play is today  uh like you know you're absolutely right  to talk about  uh sort of police officers teachers of  course  so the phase one uh that i am very much  focused on delivering  is the categories that the joint  committee on vaccination and  immunization have said they've looked at  all  cohorts you know in terms of um  different  uh professions teachers and and police  but actually  the relationship with age and of course  those who are clinically  uh vulnerable to death from the virus  are the ones that we're focused on  because if we can take them out of  harm's way if we can  protect them with the vaccine by  mid-february  if beyond that within two weeks they  would actually get that immunity  then we would have cut the deaths down  by 80 plus percent  and of course once we get through the  whole nine categories that's 99  of deaths some police officers of course  and teachers will actually get the  vaccine because they are  in those categories but we will very  quickly move on to  those other uh critical workers in the  economy  and of course those who are doing an  incredible job like our police  men and women in protecting us and of  course enforcing  the rules at the moment uh will also be  in that  the highest category of phase two  uh mr zawi we will no doubt speak again  very soon for now though thanks very  much indeed thank you

